<PageLayout
  title="Source Policy | Cooked Finance"
  description="How Cooked Finance selects, interprets, cites, versions, and reviews home energy, maintenance, safety, and financial sources."
  path="/source-policy/"
  eyebrow="Evidence"
  heading="The source should fit the claim."
  lead="A label rule, safety boundary, maintenance cadence, and consumer decision do not all belong to the same kind of source."
  updated="August 31, 2026"
>
  <h2>Preferred source hierarchy</h2>
  <ol><li>Federal statutes, regulations, test procedures, certified-product datasets, recalls, and agency guidance.</li><li>State and local public-safety agencies when jurisdiction matters.</li><li>University extension and public-service material.</li><li>The exact manufacturer manual for model-specific operation and maintenance.</li><li>Manufacturer-neutral technical sources when primary material does not answer the claim.</li></ol>
  <h2>Core U.S. sources</h2><ul><li>FTC EnergyGuide requirements and consumer explanations</li><li>DOE test procedures and purchasing guidance</li><li>ENERGY STAR certified-product records</li><li>EIA electricity and natural-gas data</li><li>EPA WaterSense and refrigerant rules</li><li>CPSC recalls and safety publications</li><li>USDA food-temperature guidance where refrigerator or freezer safety is involved</li></ul>
  <h2>Interpreting metrics</h2><p>Efficiency ratios such as CEER, CEF, UEF, IEF, IMEF, and IWF are not silently converted into annual kWh. A standardized annual-use figure is labeled as an estimate. Washer and dishwasher figures disclose modeled hot-water energy to reduce double counting.</p>
  <h2>Citations and review dates</h2><p>System pages link directly to their source set and display a substantive review date. A source link is not an endorsement of every statement on the external site. When a dataset is versioned or frequently updated, the page should identify the relevant retrieval or review date.</p>
  <h2>Claims we avoid</h2><ul><li>Guaranteed savings, lifespan, safety, reliability, or repair frequency.</li><li>A universal maintenance interval where the manual, design, use, climate, dust, or water quality controls it.</li><li>A national average price presented as the user’s rate.</li><li>Unreviewed model-number, recall, or parts data copied from another commercial site.</li></ul>
  <h2>Financial archive</h2><p>Legacy money calculators and the original financial check keep their own official finance sources and separate <a href="/money-tools/financial-health-check/methodology/">model methodology</a>.</p>
